Hmmm. Well, I think having an alignment is good because it forces you to play
your character properly - without it we could just "do what we wanted to" which
is not right when playing good characters at least.

Having a AD&D system with nine alignments (Lawful Good, Lawful Neutral, Lawful
Evil, Neutral Good etc) would be too complicated (and impossible to implement I
think). Maybe the alternative could be 5 alignments:
 Lawful, Good, Neutral, Chaotic & Evil
Although even this might be a bit difficult to work into the game properly.
